3. Task 1.2 — Thread Oversubscription Sweep

Objective

The objective was to investigate how increasing the number of software threads affects the time required to create, execute, and join the thread team.

The following thread counts were tested:

1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, and 64 threads.

Experimental Results

Threads	Time (ms)
1	10.856
2	0.465
4	0.540
8	0.982
16	1.447
32	1.339
64	4.102

Analysis

The shortest measured time was obtained with two threads at 0.465 ms. Increasing the number of threads beyond two did not continuously improve performance.

The execution time increased to 0.540 ms with four threads, 0.982 ms with eight threads, and 4.102 ms with 64 threads.

The result demonstrates that adding more software threads does not automatically result in better performance. When the thread count becomes large, thread-management and scheduling overhead become increasingly important.

The one-thread result was 10.856 ms, which is significantly higher than the two-thread result. This measurement also includes JVM and ForkJoinPool startup overhead, so it should not be interpreted as a pure measurement of computational speedup.

Question 1.2 — Oversubscription

When the number of software threads exceeds the available physical CPU resources, the operating system must share CPU execution time between multiple threads. This introduces additional scheduling and context-switching overhead. A large number of active threads can also increase cache pressure and reduce the efficiency of cache usage.

Therefore, adding more threads does not necessarily improve performance. In the experiment, the execution time increased from 0.540 ms with four threads to 4.102 ms with 64 threads.

