7. Overall Discussion

The experiments demonstrate the importance of synchronization in parallel numerical computation.

The naive shared accumulation produced incorrect values of π when multiple threads were used. This occurred because concurrent updates to the shared accumulator created a race condition.

Synchronization through a critical section can solve the correctness problem, but it introduces performance overhead because only one thread can access the protected section at a time.

Parallel reduction provides a better approach because each thread can perform part of the computation and the partial results can then be combined. However, the available parallelism is still limited by hardware resources and parallelization overhead.

The initial strong-scaling results showed that increasing the number of threads beyond four did not significantly improve execution time. This indicates that the workload reached a point where additional parallelism provided little benefit.

⸻

8. Conclusion

This laboratory demonstrated three important aspects of parallel programming: correctness, synchronization, and scalability.

The race-condition experiment showed that unsynchronized shared variables can produce highly inaccurate numerical results. With one thread, the calculated value of π was accurate, while multiple threads produced substantial errors.

The critical-section experiment is designed to demonstrate how synchronization can restore correctness while introducing additional execution overhead.

The parallel-reduction experiment demonstrated that parallel execution can reduce computation time, but the improvement is not proportional to the number of threads. The initial measurements showed the shortest execution time at four threads, while additional threads produced little or no improvement.

Overall, the experiments demonstrate that effective parallel programming requires a balance between parallelism, synchronization overhead, and available hardware resources.
